<article>
  <div class="article-header">
    <h1>Are Mushrooms Legal in Maine?</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">It depends on the mushroom. Psilocybin is illegal; Amanita muscaria is not scheduled. Here is the exact 2026 law.</p>
    <div class="byline">
      <p>By <a href="/about/authors#calvin-waters"><strong>Calvin Waters</strong></a>, Licensing &amp; Compliance Analyst. <strong>Reviewed by <a href="/about/authors#margaret-finch">Margaret Finch</a>, Finance &amp; Taxation Analyst.</strong></p>
      <p class="last-updated">Published July 21, 2026</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="answer-capsule"><strong>It depends on which mushroom you mean.</strong> Psilocybin mushrooms ("magic mushrooms") are <strong>illegal in Maine</strong> — psilocybin is a Schedule X controlled substance under <a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/17-a/title17-Asec1102.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">17-A M.R.S. §1102</a>, and possession is a Class D crime punishable by up to 364 days in jail and a $2,000 fine. But <strong>Amanita muscaria</strong> mushrooms — which contain muscimol, not psilocybin — are not scheduled under Maine or federal law and are sold commercially in most states. The two are chemically and legally distinct, and that distinction is the source of most confusion.</p>

  <section>
    <h2>The Two Kinds of "Mushroom" People Ask About</h2>
    <p>When people search "are mushrooms legal in Maine," they are usually asking about one of two very different things. Getting them mixed up leads to bad legal conclusions.</p>
    <table aria-label="Psilocybin vs Amanita muscaria legal status in Maine 2026">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Psilocybin Mushrooms</th><th>Amanita Muscaria</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Active compound</td><td>Psilocybin / psilocin</td><td>Muscimol / ibotenic acid</td></tr>
        <tr><td>How it acts</td><td>Serotonin (5-HT2A) — classic psychedelic</td><td>GABA system — sedative, dream-like</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maine status</td><td>Schedule X (17-A M.R.S. §1102)</td><td>Not scheduled</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Federal status</td><td>Schedule I</td><td>Not scheduled</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Possession in Maine</td><td>Class D crime</td><td>Not prohibited by scheduling</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sold commercially?</td><td>No (illegal)</td><td>Yes, in most states</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The rest of this page covers both, because the law treats them completely differently.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Psilocybin Mushrooms: Illegal in Maine</h2>
    <p>Psilocybin and psilocin — the psychoactive compounds in Psilocybe "magic" mushrooms — are controlled substances at both the state and federal level.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Maine:</strong> Schedule X controlled substance under <a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/17-a/title17-Asec1102.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">17-A M.R.S. §1102</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Possession penalty:</strong> Class D crime — up to 364 days in jail and a $2,000 fine.</li>
      <li><strong>Federal:</strong> Schedule I under the Controlled Substances Act.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This status has <strong>not</strong> changed as a result of any recent federal or state action. The April 2026 federal executive order on psychedelics directs the FDA to fast-track therapeutic review of certain substances; it does not reschedule psilocybin or change its legal status in Maine.</p>
  </section>

  <Callout title="The April 2026 Executive Order Does Not Legalize Psilocybin" type="warning">
    The executive order signed in April 2026 directs the FDA to expedite review of psychedelic substances for medical use. It is a regulatory-process directive, not a rescheduling. Psilocybin remains Schedule I federally and Schedule X in Maine. Anyone telling you the order "legalized mushrooms" is mistaken.
  </Callout>

  <section>
    <h2>Amanita Muscaria: Not Scheduled</h2>
    <p>Amanita muscaria — the iconic red-and-white-spotted mushroom — is a different organism with different chemistry. Its active compounds, muscimol and ibotenic acid, are not psilocybin and are not listed on any DEA schedule or in Maine's controlled-substances tables. That is why Amanita muscaria products (gummies, tinctures, capsules) are sold openly in most U.S. states.</p>
    <p>Two important caveats:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Not scheduled is not the same as approved.</strong> The FDA has stated that Amanita muscaria and its constituents do not meet the safety standard for use in food or dietary supplements. Products are typically sold as novelty or ceremonial items, not as foods or drugs.</li>
      <li><strong>Read the label.</strong> A legal Amanita product lists muscimol or Amanita muscaria extract. If a "mushroom gummy" contains psilocybin, it is illegal in Maine regardless of where it was sold.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stat-block">
    <h3>Maine Mushroom Legality — 2026 Snapshot</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Psilocybin (Maine):</strong> Schedule X, 17-A M.R.S. §1102</li>
      <li><strong>Psilocybin possession:</strong> Class D crime — up to 364 days, $2,000 fine</li>
      <li><strong>Psilocybin (federal):</strong> Schedule I</li>
      <li><strong>Amanita muscaria:</strong> not scheduled (Maine or federal)</li>
      <li><strong>LD 1034 commission report due:</strong> November 4, 2026</li>
      <li><strong>Current legal status of psilocybin:</strong> unchanged — still illegal</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What Maine Is Studying: LD 1034</h2>
    <p>Maine is not legalizing psilocybin, but it is studying the question. LD 1034 created the Commission to Study Pathways for Creating a Psilocybin Services Program in Maine. The commission is examining:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The scientific evidence on psilocybin for mental health and end-of-life care;</li>
      <li>How Oregon and Colorado created regulated psilocybin services programs;</li>
      <li>What steps Maine would need to take to establish a similar program.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The commission must submit its report — including findings, recommendations, and any proposed legislation — by <strong>November 4, 2026</strong>. The Legislature could act on those recommendations in 2027, but that is a separate political question. Nothing in this process changes the current law: psilocybin possession remains a crime in Maine today.</p>
    <p>For the full legislative history, the commission's mandate, and a realistic timeline, see our <a href="/blog/maine-psilocybin-2026-guide">Maine psilocybin 2026 monitoring guide</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <Callout title="This Is a Monitoring Situation, Not a Prediction" type="info">
    The policy environment is shifting, but the law has not. If you are a Maine cannabis operator watching the psilocybin space, treat this as monitoring content. Do not build a business plan around psilocybin services in Maine — nothing is legal, licensed, or guaranteed.
  </Callout>

